\import{fnip-macros}
\import{lean-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Dynamical system}
\date{2026-11-25}

\p{A \newvocab{dynamical system} is a triple
#{\{T, X, \varphi^{t}\}}.
The \newvocab{time set} #{T} indexes the family. The
\newvocab{state space} #{X} is a set. Each
\newvocab{evolution operator}
#{\varphi^{t}: X \to X}
is a map defined for #{t \in T}, on whatever part of #{X} the time
#{t} reaches. The same map is also written #{\varphi_{t}}.}

\p{The family satisfies two identities wherever both sides are defined.
##{\varphi^{0} = \mathrm{id}_{X},
   \qquad
   \varphi^{t+s} = \varphi^{t} \circ \varphi^{s}.}
The first says that time zero does not move a state. The second says
that evolving for time #{s} and then for time #{t} agrees with evolving
once for time #{t+s}. In the continuous-time case the family is called
a \newvocab{flow}.}

\p{\citet{Definition 1.1, §1.1.4, p. 7}{kuznetsov2004elements} is the
triple. \citet{(DS.0) and (DS.1), p. 6}{kuznetsov2004elements} are the
two identities, including the proviso that both sides be defined.
\citet{p. 5}{kuznetsov2004elements} calls the continuous-time family a
flow. Kuznetsov writes #{\varphi^{t}}; the subscript is only a change
of notation.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Flow}, read at commit
\code{8fc0483a875e84b83c6cdb6f5c124bcbb3530370},
is a jointly continuous action of a topological monoid. The
operator is defined at every time of that monoid. The theorems
\code{map_zero_apply} and \code{map_add} are the two identities
under those hypotheses.}
